fact WorkItem(id: "WI-560", description: some(value: "ListLiteral [h|t] tail dropped at occurrence-build (surfaced by WI-559). A head-tail list literal [a, b | t] parses to a ListLiteral term with heads in pos_args and the tail in a 'tail:' named arg (convert.rs CollectionLiteral build ~load 1469-1493 / parse). The term->occurrence builder (node_occurrence.rs visit_fn 'ListLiteral' arm ~3915-3927) reads pos_args only and DROPS the 'tail:' named arg, because Expr::ListLit (node_occurrence.rs:688) is a flat Vec with NO tail slot; reify (try_occurrence_to_term ListLit arm ~2330-2333, occ_build_fn with empty named_args) likewise emits no tail. CONSEQUENCE: the occurrence view of [a | t] is just [a], diverging from the SLD/resolver view -- bounded_list_elements (resolve.rs ~1100-1108) DOES chase the ListLiteral 'tail:' arg, so it sees a AND everything in t. Two concrete harms: (1) lossy round-trip -- [a | t] -> occurrence -> term yields ListLiteral(a), breaking the WI-425 occurrence<->term isomorphism for this shape; (2) latent dedup collision -- distinct source literals [a] and [a | t] both build the identical Expr::ListLit([a]) occurrence, so any occurrence cache/key (e.g. cached_term) silently aliases semantically different lists. Today this is LATENT not active: in a List-typed context the WI-007 loader desugar (convert_term_with_expected, load.rs:5881; List-only trigger find_list_element_type load.rs:5811; rewrite build_list_with_tail load.rs:4649 which threads the tail into the cons spine terminator CORRECTLY) turns [a|t] into a proper cons(a, t) spine -- which has a tail slot, so nothing is lost. The tail-bearing ListLiteral term only survives un-desugared where there is no List-typed context (e.g. a bounded-quant collection slot, the case WI-027 added). REJECTED FIX (per project owner): do NOT desugar [h|t] to cons/nil at occurrence-build time. Collection literals are TYPE-DIRECTED -- the same ListLiteral(h, tail: t) is meant to desugar to whatever collection the expected type names (List -> cons/nil today; conceptually Set/Stream via Collection.add/empty + Iteration.split per proposal 011 OQ3.4, parameterized by an effect set so effectful Stream literals work; see docs/kernel-language.md:430-432, docs/design/kernel-vocab-provenance.md:110-132). The occurrence builder has no expected-type context, so hardcoding cons/nil there would bake in List semantics and be wrong for non-List collections (and note: SetLiteral is NEVER loader-desugared today -- no Set branch in convert_term_with_expected -- it stays SetLiteral, exactly the WI-559 path). PREREQUISITE INVESTIGATION: understand how type-dependent collection-literal desugaring works end to end before changing the representation -- map convert_term_with_expected (load.rs:5881) + find_list_element_type (only List-shaped triggers) + build_list_with_tail; the typer TypeBuildFrame::ListLit/SetLit (typing.rs:4717/4753) which TYPES a literal as List[T]/Set[T] but does NOT spine-desugar; proposal 011 OQ3.4 + WI-007/WI-285/WI-393 history; and where (if anywhere) the broader Collection/Iteration desugar is meant to live vs the current List-only loader special-case. RECOMMENDED FIX DIRECTION (a): give Expr::ListLit an optional tail slot (e.g. tail: Option<Rc<NodeOccurrence>>) and thread it through build (visit_fn 'ListLiteral'), reify (try_occurrence_to_term), the printer (persistence/print.rs Expr::ListLit ~407), for_each_child + drain_expr_children (~265/983), and the typer ListLit frame -- keeping the literal carrier-neutral and faithful to the term, and leaving type-directed desugaring at its proper site (the loader / typer). Acceptance: a [a | t] head-tail list literal round-trips through occurrence_to_term WITHOUT dropping the tail (the round-trip term equals the original); [a] and [a | t] build DISTINCT occurrences (no dedup collision); cargo-test green; add a focused round-trip test."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-06-24T12:31:52Z"))

fact Tag(workitem: "WI-560", name: "wi027-followup")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-560", author: "user", content: "RESOLVED BY REMOVAL, not by the originally-filed 'carry the tail faithfully' fix (direction (a)). Root finding from a design pass with the project owner: the [h|t] head-tail surface is PARSE-ONLY with zero end-to-end semantics and zero real usage (only parse_test assertions + grammar corpus + docs; stdlib destructures lists via explicit cons(?h,?t) / case cons(h,t)). Two load-bearing facts drove the removal: (1) at PARSE time we cannot know whether a collection literal is in rule-head (pattern) or value (construction) position, so the tail-bearing ListLiteral is a position-agnostic surface that the parser commits to before the role is known; (2) the meaning of [h|t] is opposite by role -- construction (insert/cons) in value position vs destructuring (Iteration.split) in pattern position -- and split is a destructor that NEVER belongs in list-literal/expression position. For LIST, cons/nil already serves both construction and unification-destructuring, so the sugar was pure redundancy. REMOVAL (this delivery): grammar.js collection_literal drops the '| tail' alternative; convert.rs drops has_tail plumbing (build arm now alloc_fn_term, like SetLiteral); load.rs folds build_list_with_tail into build_list (no tail param) and the ListLiteral desugar drops the tail read; resolve.rs bounded_list_elements ListLiteral arm drops the tail chase; reflect.anthill + kernel-language.md 4.6 revised (destructure via cons; head-tail removed); parse_test head-tail tests replaced with head_tail_literal_surface_removed (| is a plain infix op, so [?h|?t] now parses as ListLiteral(or(?h,?t)), NOT a head-tail); tree-sitter corpus head-tail cases removed. Proper first-class type-directed collection deconstruction patterns (the real feature: [h|t]/[]/[a,b] as PATTERNS desugaring to Iteration.split for abstract carriers / cons for List, effectful for Stream, with the logical-vs-operational boundary) filed separately as WI-561 (PreOpened, tag collection-patterns). Tree-sitter 170/0; anthill-core full suite green (1908).", at: "2026-06-24T12:20:24Z")
